// JavaScript Execution Context (Js is single thread)

// browser and node.js execution are different. (each env has different Execution-Context)

//  {#code} -> global EC -> this
//      |->  Global EC
//      |->  Functional EC

// {#code}
//      |->  Memory creation phase
//      |->  Execution phase

let val1 = 100
let val2 = 20
function addTwo(num1, num2){
    let total = num1+num2
    return total
}
let result1 = addTwo(val1, val2)
let result2 = addTwo(18, 7)

// how the above code will execute will explain below step by step
1. Global Execution -> this
2. Memory phase -> val1 = undefined; val2 = undefined; addTwo = defination; result1 = undefined; result2=undefined; {also called first-cycle}

3. Execution phase -> val1 = 100; val2=20; {function allready defined} 
at line no 19: addTwo=> will create seprate context/Execution context as=> {new variable env + Execution thread}
for each function they will again do Memory & execution phase at seprate context/Execution context
i) Memory phase(we have to do with function defination) -> val1=undefined; val2=undefined; total=undefined; 
ii) Execution phase -> val1=100; val2=20; total=120;
*{in function return basicaly returning value into Global context}
* after both phase the seprate context/Execution context(for function) will delete also after execution phase get the value of that function

3. Execution phase -> val1 = 100; val2=20; result1 = 120;

// Call-Stack

* In call-stack firstly Global-Execution will happend then function will in and out one by one
* if function have nested function's then LiFo concept comes into the pitcure which stands for: Last-in-First-out.